#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048780 :topic/difficulty 1.9138196583074114 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428104 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1106->1102. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050467} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "be10c23c-41f3-53b5-9245-d50fb098c325" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096451 :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](images/b8/b8abf22d14f93a38c4d01f368b8171e4ccfe90086d932f5b58453baa46391405.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/76/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{m} & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096653 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096452 :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/math-academy-id "e-4288" :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}.$\n\n![](images/67/678ea2936b8a5b1ded74cb8a0875c629e8be0f7002c98492ebf2e8109fc603ef.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096495 :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5212" :question/problem "Consider the picture below.\n\n![](images/f2/f2518c2e998e7c7f551548a4cc7283720a66428ea8fa9872494a81cc3848ba9e.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when two known vectors are not parallel, a linear combination of them is unique. So, once we rewrite $\\mathbf c$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b,$ we can match coefficients to determine the unknown scalar.\n\nTo find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2 \\mathbf{b} .$ So, we first draw the vector $2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea517ac4ca8e0bcf7ba48f23e66f100323d5485f1221219aacfbea9148f4a1af.png)\n\nNote that in $△ O D C$, we have:\n\n- $D C = 2 | \\mathbf{a} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{D C}$ and $\\mathbf a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{D C} = 2 \\mathbf{a} .$\n\nAdditionally,\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overrightarrow{O D} + \\overrightarrow{D C} \\\\  &  = 2 \\mathbf{b} + 2 \\mathbf{a} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} = 2 \\mathbf{b} + 2 \\mathbf{a} .$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get:\n$$\n\\lambda=2\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234976 :question/id #uuid "aeaa36a2-41b5-5b7b-9797-f39df1e34050" :question/math-academy-id "e-26144" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d4/d4f18c3567d0c55d3d6e002fbe2c6ac50b97b7adc14b016c8e95006de98ca15d.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} ,$ find $\\lambda.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $- 2 \\mathbf{b} .$ So, we first draw the vector $- 2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/65/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $- 2 \\mathbf{b} .$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} .\n$$\n\nIn $△ O P C ,$ we have:\n\n- $O P = 2 | \\mathbf{b} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ and $\\mathbf b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O P} = - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- $P C = | \\mathbf{a} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{P C}$ and $\\mathbf a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{P C} = - \\mathbf{a} .\n$$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} \\\\  &  = - 2 \\mathbf{b} + ( - \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\  &  = - \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} = - \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1106 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :topic/title "Describing the Position Vector of a Point Using Known Vectors"}]}
